id "q-332952/field-1/ma-v1/5478d3a718291116bc7aed279d798f5bd070a86d9b108fd6b8a43af83600a503" :answer-field/id #uuid "0d0b049d-b68e-502e-a989-9e7aad596a89" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-332952/field-1/ma-v1/5478d3a718291116bc7aed279d798f5bd070a86d9b108fd6b8a43af83600a503/ae17e2311ebdcb443976f856340f688b68e78591b3ccdd9c5d8ffe3ef1675120" :answer-field/choices ["q-332952/field-1/ma-v1/5478d3a718291116bc7aed279d798f5bd070a86d9b108fd6b8a43af83600a503/5f8e96fcef1cbe01e6835a0848fdcdb5b4ddc544a7a4da93a09510fb4372f8f7" "q-332952/field-1/ma-v1/5478d3a718291116bc7aed279d798f5bd070a86d9b108fd6b8a43af83600a503/590e223dd2b8bc7487dd03750c9d33f095cb4f45e3bb1a22990cb31f166d0d54" "q-332952/field-1/ma-v1/5478d3a718291116bc7aed279d798f5bd070a86d9b108fd6b8a43af83600a503/ae17e2311ebdcb443976f856340f688b68e78591b3ccdd9c5d8ffe3ef1675120" "q-332952/field-1/ma-v1/5478d3a718291116bc7aed279d798f5bd070a86d9b108fd6b8a43af83600a503/3eedad2b68f9e406204290c223fc7f33368e09cf74b8e7e09666a6fecaf84e6a" "q-332952/field-1/ma-v1/5478d3a718291116bc7aed279d798f5bd070a86d9b108fd6b8a43af83600a503/47660004af8c7eb8cb3de5f83042d3129092485a315696ee6f14551021a20f8e" "q-332952/field-1/ma-v1/5478d3a718291116bc7aed279d798f5bd070a86d9b108fd6b8a43af83600a503/0dbbee0924d29cddc95e729e19738a391c447170486ca6319bb5fb581d916120"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186164414 :answer-field/correct 17592186164417 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-332952"] :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=x(t)-4y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=9x(t)+y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $\\lambda =1\\pm 6\\text{i}.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.$\\,$\nSince \n{{field-1}},\nthe equilibrium point (origin) is {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "Let $v=p+q\\text{i},$ where $p,q\\in {R}^{2},$ be an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =a+b\\text{i}.$\n\nThen, a complex solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{complex}}(t)=v{e}^{\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nUsing Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{complex}}(t) & =v{e}^{(a+b\\text{i})t} \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt))(p+q\\text{i}) \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)p-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)q)+\\text{i}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)q).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\text{x}(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)p-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)q)+{c}_{2}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)q),\n$$\n\nwhere the parts $(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)p-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)q)$ and $(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)q)$ are bounded.\n\nNow, notice that $a=\\text{Re}(\\lambda )=1>0$ and ${e}^{at}={e}^{t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty .$ Thus, we have a spiral that is traversed toward infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$ \n\nTherefore, since $\\text{Re}(\\lambda )>0,$ the equilibrium point (origin) is $\\text{an unstable spiral (source)}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-332952/field-1/ma-v1/5478d3a718291116bc7aed279d798f5bd070a86d9b108fd6b8a43af83600a503"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-334273/field-1/51b074d361295bee790177e009ace63dda2f2748ac6f116b20b3ebc44c60c701" :answer/id #uuid "f90db81d-0268-52f6-b23c-2fc9c698cf75" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "unbounded" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334273/field-1/c8be2b294e3b6d306f2972d4d44dded126ca9657f32ccd9c9b94897d3378ccf5" :answer/id #uuid "073b71f8-7c65-52bd-965e-5cbaefe4b927" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "bounded" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334273/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "df85c79d-21f7-5844-9b5f-3cb7ee09c9bc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/t